  "query_preview": "..Another day of deep moisture across the region will\nbring an elevated threat of flash flooding. While most of the\nregion is seeing conditions favorable for heavy rain associated\nwith thunderstorms, the highest chance will continue across\neastern and southern Utah. Some areas, including Castle Country,\nthe Sevier and Sanpete Valleys, the San Rafael Swell and most of\nthe Grand Staircase area have already had one day of heavy rain\nand flooding. With saturated soils, these areas will be even more\nprone to flooding this afternoon and evening.\n\nAs far as Monday, PW anomalies at or above 97-99 percentile will\nshift into southern and eastern Utah. This...combined with\nwidespread large scale ascent, significant deep layer shear and \nsufficient instability will support widespread thunderstorms with\nvery heavy rain. Average return intervals (ARI) for precipitation\nare exceeding 10-20 years across much of eastern and southern \nUtah, with localized areas seeing maximum ARIs in excess of 50\nyears. Rainfall rates in previous days have exceeded 0.30 inches\nin 10 minutes in stronger thunderstorms. Given this, coordinated\nwith the Weather Prediction Center to introduce a moderate risk\nwithin the Excessive Rainfall Outlook (ERO) for Monday. This will\nbe only the second time WFO SLC has been included in a day 2 \nmoderate risk ERO.\n\nMuch drier conditions will work into the region in the wake of the\nshortwave trough that spurred Monday's convection as drier\nsouthwest flow filters into the area. That said...this break from\nflash flooding is short as a number of ensemble members are\nsuggesting tropical moisture from an eastern Pacific tropical\ncyclone approaching the Baja California area may shift into Utah\nahead of another trough by Friday into Saturday. Something to\nmonitor.",
